Actually the known Express behavior: `trust proxy` number = "trust the nth hop from the front-facing proxy server as the client". Express docs: "trust proxy: 1 — trust the first hop from the front-facing proxy server as the client". Hmm, actually the docs say:

> `app.set('trust proxy', 1)` // trust first proxy

And the semantics: number of hops. With `1`, the socket address is trusted (the LB), and the client is the last XFF entry. So `req.ip` should be `<real address>`.

But the report says req.ip comes out as 6.6.6.6. So the trust function `i <= 1` trusts index 0 and index 1. Index 1 in the addrs array (after reverse and push socket) is the last XFF entry = real address. So it's trusted, and then index 2 = 6.6.6.6 is untrusted, so req.ip = 6.6.6.6. That's the bug: off-by-one. Should be `i < val` i.e. `i <= val - 1`.

