Interesting — the code already looks like the "fixed" version. So the bug must be in `compileTrust` in lib/utils.js: `return function(a, i){ return i <= val }`. With val = 1, this trusts index 0 and 1. proxyaddr's `alladdrs` builds addrs = [socket, ...forwarded reversed]. Let's recall proxy-addr implementation:

```js
function alladdrs(req, trust) {
  var addrs = forwarded(req)
  if (!trust) return addrs
  if (typeof trust !== 'function') trust = compile(trust)
  for (var i = 0; i < addrs.length - 1; i++) {
    if (trust(addrs[i], i)) continue
    addrs.length = i + 1
  }
  return addrs
}
```

And `forwarded(req)` returns [socket.remoteAddress, ...XFF reversed]. So addrs[0] = socket address (the load balancer), addrs[1] = last XFF entry (real client), addrs[2] = spoofed 6.6.6.6.

With trust = i <= 1: i=0 trusted (LB), i=1 trusted (real client) → loop continues to i=2? Loop condition i < addrs.length - 1 = 2, so i=0,1 only. So addrs stays length 3. Then proxyaddr returns addrs[addrs.length-1] = 6.6.6.6. Bug!

